    "https://www.ohiouniversityjobs.com/postings/58017": {
      "title": "Associate Dean, Academic Affairs - Medical Education and Open Rank Professor - Heritage College of Osteopathic Medicine",
      "description": "Ohio University - Job Site | Associate Dean, Academic Affairs - Medical Education and Open Rank Professor - Heritage College of Osteopathic Medicine Skip to Main Content Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Job Alerts Log In /Create Account Help Take a virtual tour of OHIO Explore Athens County Ohio University Home Office of Civil Rights Compliance Ohio University Human Resources Total Compensation Estimator Student Employment Dual Career Network Equal Employment and Educational Opportunity Policy Notice of Non-Discrimination Annual Security and Fire Safety Report OH/Western PA/WV Higher Education Recruitment Consortium HERC Associate Dean, Academic Affairs - Medical Education and Open Rank Professor - Heritage College of Osteopathic Medicine Below you will find the details for the position including any supplementary documentation and questions you should review before applying for the opening. To apply for the position, please click the Apply for this Job link/button. If you would like to bookmark this position for later review, click on the Bookmark link. If you would like to print a copy of this position for your records, click on the Print Preview link. Applicant Portal Voluntary Demographics. The information requested below is used to assist us in our compliance with Federal/State equal employment opportunity record keeping and reporting. Your response is voluntary and will not be used in any way to determine your eligibility for employment. Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Please see Special Instructions for more details. Please complete the online application and attach the required documents. Required documents include a detailed cover letter, current resume, and a list of at least three (3) professional references with current contact details, including email addresses. The position will remain open until filled . For full consideration, please apply by 11:59 p.m. on November 29, 2026. Posting Details Applications will be accepted until midnight (Eastern Time Zone) on the ‘Posting End Date’ shown below. Position Information Business Title Associate Dean, Academic Affairs - Medical Education and Open Rank Professor - Heritage College of Osteopathic Medicine Applications Accepted From Public: Open to the Public Special Instructions to Applicants Please complete the online application and attach the required documents. Required documents include a detailed cover letter, current resume, and a list of at least three (3) professional references with current contact details, including email addresses. The position will remain open until filled . For full consideration, please apply by 11:59 p.m. on November 29, 2026. This position is eligible for the following benefits: medical, vision, dental, basic and supplemental life, winter break closure, paid holidays, educational benefits for you and eligible dependents, sick leave, vacation, and retirement. Posting Number 2016562T Job Description Summary Department Summary About the Heritage College of Osteopathic Medicine: Mission: We pride ourselves on our strong focus on our mission: training osteopathic primary care physicians to serve Ohio. Our medical school educates physicians committed to practice in Ohio, emphasizes primary care, engages in focused research, and embraces both Appalachian and urban communities. Integral to this mission, our college community commits itself to: providing a clinically integrated, learning-centered, osteopathic medical education continuum for students, interns, residents and primary care associates; embracing public service; and improving the health and well-being of underserved populations. Vision: A healthier Ohio, empowered by compassionate osteopathic physicians. With our graduates and partners, we advance care and knowledge to improve the health of our communities. Our culture is built upon resiliency, courage and compassion. Our physicians humanize each patient encounter, bridging the gap between therapeutics, medical technologies, health systems, care delivery and disparity. Essential Values: These fundamental principles form the foundation of the culture at our college: wholeness and balance within each person; integrity; community of mutual respect; acceptance of others; pursuit of excellence; a climate of scholarship; and commitment to service, generosity and compassion. Our Campuses: https://www.ohio.edu/medicine/about/campuses Why Choose Ohio University? https://www.ohio.edu/hr/jobs/working-ohio https://www.ohio.edu/hr/benefits About the Office of Medical Education https://www.ohio.edu/medicine/med-education About the Office of Clinical Education https://www.ohio.edu/medicine/about/offices/clinical-education Position Summary: The Associate Dean, Academic Affairs – Medical Education is a strategic administrative leadership role for undergraduate medical education with a non-tenure track faculty appointment as an Open Rank Professor in one of the HCOM academic departments (Primary Care, Osteopathic Manipulative Medicine, Specialty Medicine, Biomedical Sciences, or Social Medicine). As a faculty leader, the Associate Dean contributes to the College’s missions of teaching, curriculum innovation, design, sequencing, delivery, assessment, evaluation, and continuous improvement of the non-clinical components of the Doctor of Osteopathic Medicine (DO) curriculum. The Associate Dean of Medical Education serves as the College’s lead academic administrator for non-clinical curriculum strategy, educational effectiveness, learner assessment, program evaluation, and educational innovation. The position provides academic oversight of biomedical, osteopathic, social, behavioral, population health, ethics, professionalism, and health systems sciences and ensures that students develop the foundational knowledge, skills, and competencies necessary for successful progression into clinical training. As a co-steward of the unified four-year DO curriculum, the Associate Dean works in close partnership with the Associate Dean for Academic Affairs – Clinical Education to ensure curricular coherence, vertical integration, comparability, quality, and continuous improvement across all four years, campuses, instructional modalities, and clinical training environments. The Associate Dean also collaborates closely with the Associate Dean for Quality and Accreditation to ensure that curriculum, assessment, program evaluation, and continuous quality improvement activities align with accreditation requirements, institutional learning outcomes, and College and University priorities. The Associate Dean provides academic leadership and decision-making direction while partnering with operational and administrative leaders responsible for scheduling, logistics, educational technology, and day-to-day curriculum implementation across campuses. The Associate Dean has primary academic accountability for the non-clinical curriculum and contributes to program-level academic governance and integration across the entire four-year DO program. This position is responsible for promoting consistency, comparability, integration, and educational quality in: Foundational and non-clinical curriculum content across campuses and instructional modalities Alignment of program outcomes, course and session objectives, instructional methods, and assessment strategies Integration of biomedical, osteopathic, clinical, social, behavioral, population, and health systems sciences Academic standards, learner assessment, student performance, and progression Preparation of students for successful transition to clinical education Educational innovation and evidence-informed teaching and learning Curriculum evaluation and continuous quality improvement Alignment with the College’s mission, osteopathic philosophy, accreditation standards, and institutional priorities Curriculum Leadership and Academic Oversight Provide executive academic leadership for the design, oversight, integration, sequencing, evaluation, and continuous improvement of the pre-clerkship curriculum and associated educational programs. Provide primary academic leadership for foundational biomedical, osteopathic, social, behavioral, population health, ethics, professionalism, and health systems sciences delivered during the pre-clerkship phase. Provide strategic oversight of the longitudinal DO curriculum in collaboration with the Associate Dean for Academic Affairs – Clinical Education to ensure curricular coherence, vertical integration, quality, comparability, and innovation across all four years of the program. Lead curriculum planning, revision, implementation, evaluation, and improvement in alignment with the Heritage College Bylaws, institutional priorities, accreditation expectations, learner outcomes, and contemporary medical education practices. Collaborate with curriculum leaders, course directors, faculty, department chairs, and academic administrators to ensure effective integration and delivery of the educational program across campuses. Determine curricular and educational priorities based on institutional strategic goals, accreditation requirements, learner outcomes, national standards, and emerging educational practices. Provide academic direction and oversight to pre-clerkship curriculum leadership, including course directors, faculty leads, and curriculum liaisons, while collaborating with operational teams responsible for curriculum support and delivery. Ensure that the educational program prepares students for academic progression, successful transition to clinical education, program completion, licensure examinations, graduate medical education, and subsequent professional practice. Assessment, Student Performance, and Academic Progression Provide leadership for the development, implementation, validation, evaluation, and continuous improvement of learner assessment strategies across the pre-clerkship curriculum. Oversee written examinations, performance-based assessments, Objective Structured Clinical Examinations (OSCEs), and other assessment modalities designed to evaluate student achievement of curricular and program outcomes. Apply and promote sound psychometric principles to assessment development, analysis, standard setting, score interpretation, reliability, validity, and fairness. Oversee analysis and interpretation of learner assessment data, academic performance indicators, licensure examination performance, and national benchmarking data to identify opportunities for curricular and academic improvement. Collaborate with Student Affairs, Clinical Education, and other institutional leaders regarding academic standing, student progression, remediation, promotion, board examination readiness, and graduation requirements. Ensure assessment strategies appropriately measure the knowledge, skills, behaviors, and competencies required for progression through the DO program. Curriculum Evaluation and Continuous Quality Improvement Lead systematic evaluation of the curriculum using learner outcomes, assessment results, program evaluation findings, student feedback, faculty input, accreditation findings, and national benchmarking data. Use structured assessment and continuous quality improvement frameworks to evaluate educational effectiveness and guide curricular improvement. Lead structured CQI processes that use assessment and program data to inform curricular revision, educational program development, and institutional performance improvement. Oversee curriculum mapping and related analytics to ensure alignment among program outcomes, learning objectives, instructional methods, assessments, degree requirements, and accreditation standards. Oversee collection, analysis, and reporting of curricular data through appropriate curriculum mapping and national reporting systems, including the AAMC Curriculum Inventory or comparable systems. Evaluate the impact of curricular innovations and major educational initiatives through systematic program evaluation and outcomes analysis. Educational Strategy, Innovation, and Change Management Lead strategic educational initiatives designed to advance academic excellence, student learning, curricular effectiveness, and educational innovation. Promote evidence-informed teaching practices, contemporary instructional methodologies, appropriate educational technologies, and innovative approaches to medical education. Lead major curricular change initiatives, including curriculum reform, new educational programs, tracks, electives, instructional models, and educational technology adoption. Provide leadership for the implementation and evaluation of significant curricular changes across campuses. Identify emerging trends and best practices in undergraduate medical education and assess their potential application within the Heritage College. Academic Governance and Curriculum Administration Provide leadership for curriculum governance processes related to course approvals, curricular revisions, academic policies, assessment, progression, promotion, and degree requirements. Engage regularly with the College Curriculum Committee and other faculty governance structures responsible for curriculum and academic affairs. Present proposed changes to pre-clerkship and non-clinical curriculum content, structure, sequencing, instructional methods, and assessment strategies for appropriate governance review. Provide curriculum mapping analyses, learner outcomes data, assessment findings, and CQI results to support evidence-informed governance decisions. Collaborate with the Associate Dean for Academic Affairs – Clinical Education on curricular proposals requiring College and University review and approval. Partner with the Associate Dean for Academic Affairs – Clinical Education on University Curriculum Council processes necessary to maintain the DO program, including catalog requirements, DARS , course listings, and related curricular approvals. Collaborate on development and maintenance of the annual academic and enrollment calendar. Ensure accuracy and integrity of curricular information contained in University catalogs, academic records, curriculum management systems, and related institutional documents. Accreditation and Educational Quality Collaborate with the Associate Dean for Quality and Accreditation to ensure that curricular design, delivery, assessment, evaluation, and improvement activities meet or exceed applicable accreditation and institutional standards. Support ongoing compliance with Commission on Osteopathic College Accreditation ( COCA ), Higher Learning Commission ( HLC ), University Curriculum Committee, and other applicable standards and requirements. Contribute to accreditation self-studies, reports, site visits, monitoring activities, and required curricular documentation. Use accreditation findings, educational outcomes, assessment data, and program evaluation results to inform curriculum refinement and strategic academic decision-making. Ensure appropriate evidence is maintained to demonstrate curricular integration, educational effectiveness, comparability, student achievement, and continuous quality improvement across instructional locations. Faculty Development and Academic Leadership Provide supervision, academic oversight, mentoring, and direction to designated pre-clerkship curriculum leaders, including course directors, faculty leads, and curriculum liaisons. Support and mentor faculty engaged in curriculum design, instructional delivery, learner assessment, program evaluation, and educational innovation. Promote faculty development in instructional design, teaching effectiveness, assessment methodology, educational technology, and evidence-informed medical education practices. Foster a culture of academic excellence, innovation, accountability, collaboration, and continuous improvement among curriculum leaders and faculty. Support strategies for the recruitment, development, engagement, and retention of faculty necessary to deliver a high-quality medical education program. Institutional Leadership and Collaboration Serve as a member of the College’s academic leadership structure and provide strategic advice to the Senior Dean and Chief Academic Officer regarding medical education, curriculum, student academic performance, and educational quality. Serve in an advisory or leadership capacity on key institutional committees, including the College Curriculum Committee, Dean’s Council, Executive Committee, and other committees as assigned. Collaborate closely with the Associate Dean for Academic Affairs – Clinical Education as co-leaders responsible for curricular integration and academic quality across the four-year DO program. Partner with the Associate Dean for Quality and Accreditation on assessment, program evaluation, accreditation, outcomes analysis, and continuous quality improvement. Collaborate with Student Affairs and Admissions to strengthen cross-functional processes related to student transition, academic progress, board examinations, registration, progression, and graduation requirements. Partner with department chairs, faculty leaders, campus leadership, and institutional stakeholders to align educational initiatives with College priorities. Promote effective communication and collaboration across campuses to ensure a consistent, integrated, and high-quality student learning experience. Perform other duties as assigned by the Senior Dean and Chief Academic Officer. Faculty Responsibilities Maintain an active faculty appointment and contribute to the teaching mission of the College through clinical skills instruction, learner assessment, curriculum development, and other educational activities. Participate in educational scholarship, clinical education innovation, and dissemination of best practices in medical education. Serve as a faculty leader in academic governance, curriculum oversight, and assessment decision-making. Mentor faculty, clerkship directors, and clinical preceptors and support faculty development related to teaching, assessment, feedback, and competency-based education. Contribute to institutional service and committee activities consistent with faculty expectations and responsibilities. Contribute a minimum of 5% effort to medical school teaching activities, including curriculum design and enhancement, delivery of instructional content, facilitation of active learning sessions, provision of backup teaching support when faculty coverage is needed, and development, review, and validation of assessment tools, including written examination questions and other evaluative instruments. College Curriculum Committee ( CCC ) The Associate Dean for Academic Affairs – Medical Education engages regularly with the College Curriculum Committee ( CCC ) as part of the College’s shared academic governance structure. Typical interactions include: Presenting proposed changes to foundational and non-clinical curriculum content, structure, sequencing, and assessment methods for CCC review and recommendation. Providing curriculum mapping analyses, learner outcomes data, and continuous quality improvement findings to inform CCC deliberations and decision-making. Collaborating with CCC leadership and members to address accreditation-related requirements, curricular revisions, and alignment with institutional and national standards. Partnering with the Associate Dean, Academic Affairs – Clinical Education on curricular proposals requiring CCC consideration and subsequent University Curriculum Council ( UCC ) approval, including catalog updates, degree audit requirements, and course listing changes. Position Profile Duties/essential functions may include, but not be limited to, the following: Academic leadership role with a faculty appointment in a Heritage College of Osteopathic Medicine department. Majority of time spent on administrative responsibilities with minimum 5% medical school teaching responsibilities. Level Su"
    },
    "https://www.ohiouniversityjobs.com/postings/58004": {
      "title": "MAINTENANCE REPAIR WORKER 4",
      "description": "Ohio University - Job Site | MAINTENANCE REPAIR WORKER 4 Skip to Main Content Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Job Alerts Log In /Create Account Help Take a virtual tour of OHIO Explore Athens County Ohio University Home Office of Civil Rights Compliance Ohio University Human Resources Total Compensation Estimator Student Employment Dual Career Network Equal Employment and Educational Opportunity Policy Notice of Non-Discrimination Annual Security and Fire Safety Report OH/Western PA/WV Higher Education Recruitment Consortium HERC MAINTENANCE REPAIR WORKER 4 Below you will find the details for the position including any supplementary documentation and questions you should review before applying for the opening. To apply for the position, please click the Apply for this Job link/button. If you would like to bookmark this position for later review, click on the Bookmark link. If you would like to print a copy of this position for your records, click on the Print Preview link. Applicant Portal Voluntary Demographics. The information requested below is used to assist us in our compliance with Federal/State equal employment opportunity record keeping and reporting. Your response is voluntary and will not be used in any way to determine your eligibility for employment. Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Please see Special Instructions for more details. This position is open to the public. However, current Ohio University AFSCME 1699 bargaining unit employees will be given first consideration. CURRENT OHIO UNIVERSITY EMPLOYEES MUST INCLUDE THEIR OHIO UNIVERSITY EMPLOYMENT HISTORY AND ANY RELEVANT VERIFIABLE EXPERIENCE TO SHOW THEY MEET THE MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS FOR THIS CLASSIFICATION. IF YOUR APPLICATION IS INCOMPLETE, YOU WILL BE SCREENED OUT. IF YOU NEED YOUR OHIO UNIVERSITY EMPLOYMENT HISTORY OR NEED ASSISTANCE COMPLETING THE APPLICATION, PLEASE CONTACT UNIVERSITY HUMAN RESOURCES FOR ASSISTANCE. • We seek candidates with a commitment to working effectively with students, faculty and staff. • In an effort to make the process of warding bid upon positions more efficient, the successful candidate must indicate at the time of offer whether they accept or decline the position. • Candidates may contact management during the posting period to ask questions regarding the expectations of the position Posting Details Applications will be accepted until midnight (Eastern Time Zone) on the ‘Posting End Date’ shown below. Position Information Job Title MAINTENANCE REPAIR WORKER 4 Applications Accepted From Public: Open to the public , AFSCME 1699 Bargaining Unit Wide Special Instructions to Applicants This position is open to the public. However, current Ohio University AFSCME 1699 bargaining unit employees will be given first consideration. CURRENT OHIO UNIVERSITY EMPLOYEES MUST INCLUDE THEIR OHIO UNIVERSITY EMPLOYMENT HISTORY AND ANY RELEVANT VERIFIABLE EXPERIENCE TO SHOW THEY MEET THE MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS FOR THIS CLASSIFICATION . IF YOUR APPLICATION IS INCOMPLETE , YOU WILL BE SCREENED OUT . IF YOU NEED YOUR OHIO UNIVERSITY EMPLOYMENT HISTORY OR NEED ASSISTANCE COMPLETING THE APPLICATION , PLEASE CONTACT UNIVERSITY HUMAN RESOURCES FOR ASSISTANCE . • We seek candidates with a commitment to working effectively with students, faculty and staff. • In an effort to make the process of warding bid upon positions more efficient, the successful candidate must indicate at the time of offer whether they accept or decline the position. • Candidates may contact management during the posting period to ask questions regarding the expectations of the position This position is eligible for the following benefits: medical, vision, dental, basic and supplemental life, winter break closure, paid holidays, educational benefits for you and eligible dependents, sick leave, vacation, and retirement. Posting Number 20167066S Job Description Under general direction of lead worker or supervision by departmental administrator performs highly skilled repair, installation, maintenance work and general maintenance on buildings & equipment. Provides work direction, training & mentoring of lower-level workers. Established pattern of good work habits and performance with good interpersonal skills. Adheres to all OSHA and/or Ohio University required safety guidelines PERFORMS HIGHLY SKILLED DUTIES IN ONE OR MORE OF THE FOLLOWING CRAFT AREAS : • CARPENTRY : maintenance, alteration, &/or repair to buildings. Equipment, & wooden structures (e.g., doors, windows, walls, steps, floors, woodwork, stairways, furniture, cabinets); performs spot roof repairs; installs security devices, locks & window air conditioners. • CARPET / TILE : skilled installation, maintenance, and repair of all types of floor, ceiling and wall finishes (e.g.: carpet, padding, vinyl floor covering(s), vinyl base, wall paper, etc.) • CONTROLS : maintenance, installation, and trouble-shooting of control systems and related devices. Required to use laptop and desktop computer for DDC controls related programming and work. • ELECTRICAL : maintenance, replacement, installation & repair of electrical equipment (e.g., light fixtures, electrical outlets, switches, motors, relays, appliances, electrical circuits, emergency generators, fire alarms, intercoms, autoclaves, meters, fans, radios, televisions, electrical forklift). • ENVIRONMENTAL SERVICES : clean-up of asbestos, lead paint, mold remediation, & clean-up of other environmental hazards. • HVAC : routine installation, maintenance, and repair of heating, air conditioning, refrigeration equipment and devices. • LIFE SAFETY : maintains, tests, installs and repairs fire alarm systems and components (e.g.: smoke detectors, heat detectors, horn/strobes, flow switches, fire panels, fire extinguishers, etc.) • LOCKSMITH : installation, maintenance and repair of all types of locks and related equipment. Required to use laptop computer for electronic access troubleshooting, and programming. • MASONRY : mixing, pouring, laying and finishing concrete; repairing & replacing concrete sidewalks, floors, walls, steps and/or other similar structures; laying bricks, blocks and/or tile; sets marker stakes and making forms. • PAINTING : paints fixtures, rooms, buildings, signs & equipment; patches plaster & prepares surfaces for painting; assists in masonry work. • PLASTERING : preparing surfaces for plastering (e.g. replaces broken metal or wooden lathes, chips out broken plaster) and mixes plaster; repairs walls, floors, walks and other parts of buildings – replacing tiles, blocks and/or bricks – replacing tiles, blocks &/or bricks; patches concrete. • PLUMBING : maintenance, replacement, installation and repair of plumbing equipment and fixtures (e.g. water and steam lines, sewer pipes, plumbing fixtures, pumps, regulators, valves, faucets, toilets, sinks, bathtubs, piping). • PREVENTIVE MAINTENANCE / FILTERS : replacement & maintenance of filters, belts, sheaves, motors, shafts, bearing and other mechanical equipment; cleans filters, louvers, coils, fans, etc. • SHEET METAL : installation, fabrication and repair of metal roofs and related fixtures, fittings and metal parts. • TUNNEL MAINTENANCE : maintenance, installation & repair of utility distribution piping systems located in utility tunnels. May perform welding and related tasks. Performs machine trades functions. Operates equipment and related accessories to machine shop. • Provides work direction, training and mentoring of lower level workers in any of the above craft areas. May supervise contractors or others doing work in the above craft areas. • On continuous basis, inspects buildings & equipment for needed repairs or replacements, & performs systematic & routine preventative maintenance of assigned areas; unlocks & locks buildings. • Required to make independent decisions, as necessary. • Completes necessary documentation for Computerized Maintenance Management System ( CMMS ). • Performs other related duties as required. Minimum Qualifications • Forty-eight (48) months verifiable skilled experience in building maintenance and repair, and/or related experience in any skilled craft area(s), or equivalent; and use of associated hand & power tools and equipment; or equivalent • Journey-level working knowledge of one or more skilled craft areas • Strong knowledge of related building codes • Knowledge of employee mentoring, training and development, inventory control. • Read & write common vocabulary in sentence structure; • add, subtract, multiply & divide whole numbers, calculate fractions, decimals & percentages; • ability to apply principles to solve practical problems • Ability to carry out instructions in written, oral, control-system blue-prints, and pictorial form. Work independently or with others • Willingness to foster a welcoming and respectful workforce and community • Demonstrate dexterity and skill in use of craft-associated hand &/or power tools & equipment • Knowledge of all applicable OSHA and/or Ohio University safety guidelines and procedures • Work at heights, lift up to 50 lbs. • Successful completion of a test may be required. Valid driver’s license. In the Insulation Shop, requires obtaining CDL to transport hazardous waste in accordance with Section 4506.01 of Ohio Revised Code; requires annual training to be certified as an asbestos hazard abatement specialist as required by federal EPA • May be exposed to dirt, grease, odors, cramped conditions, extreme hot or cold temperatures, heights, and electrical hazards; may be exposed to dangerous machinery; confined spaces • An established pattern of good work habits and performance with good interpersonal skills. Preferred Qualifications Working Conditions/Physical Demands Department ZNSVL Facilities Management Pay Rate Pay range |08|: $24.62 – $27.72 Total Compensation Estimator (For Full-Time Benefits Eligible Employees) Discover the full value of joining Ohio University by clicking this link: https://www.ohio.edu/hr/compensation/total-compensation-estimator Our Total Compensation Estimator shows your offer package in dollars: salary plus university paid benefits including health coverage, retirement contributions, paid time off, tuition benefits, and more. See details on what we offer in one place and compare different options for retirement and insurance. Estimates are informational only; exact coverage is determined by eligibility policies and official plan documents. Job Open Date 10/08/2026 Posting Close Date 11/5/2026 Job Category Classified AFSCME 1699 Bargaining Unit appointment Months Planning Unit Regional Higher Education Work Schedule Current work schedule is Monday thru Friday, 7:00 AM to 3:30 PM with 30 minutes for lunch. Please note: this schedule is subject to change based on its variable appointment status. Campus Zanesville, Ohio Expected hours worked per week 40 Expected duration of assignment on-going Applicants may contact this person if they have questions about this position. Kim Hayden, hayden@ohio.edu Non-Discrimination Statement Ohio University is proud of its rich history and as part of our ongoing efforts to provide and support a transformative learning experience, we affirm our commitment to fostering a welcoming and respectful workforce and community. All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply and will receive consideration free from discrimination on the basis of age, ancestry, color, disability, ethnicity, gender, gender identity or expression, genetic information, military service or veteran status, national origin, pregnancy, race, religion, sex, sexual orientation, status as a parent or foster parent, or any other bases under the law. Ohio University is an equal access/equal opportunity employer. Job Information Employment Type full-time regular Job Family/Subfamily Career Track & Level Pay Grade AFSCME|08| Clery Act Annual Safety and Fire Report Clery Act Statistics To view the Clery Act Annual Safety and Fire Report Click Here! Applicant Documents Required Documents Optional Documents Posting Specific Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). * Please indicate how you learned about this posting. www. ohiouniversityjobs.com www.higheredjobs.com www.ohiomeansjobs.com The Athens Messenger The Chronicle of Higher Education Ohio University employee OHIO Jobs Line (phone) Employment Agency Posted Announcement Professional association Social Media (LinkedIn, Twitter, etc) OTHER * Please select an option that best represents your current status: I am a current Ohio University employee and NOT currently serving a probationary period. I am a current Ohio University employee, and I am serving in either an initial or a promotional probationary period. I am a current Ohio University Student employee. I am not an employee of Ohio University. * Are you a current Ohio University AFSCME Bargaining Unit employee who works in Zanesville Facilities Management? Yes No * Are you a current Ohio University AFSCME 1699 employee? yes no Ohio University Human Resources | Grosvenor West 103 | 1 Ohio University | Athens, OH 45701| T: 740.593.1636 | F: 740.593.0386 Ohio University | Athens OH 45701 | 740.593.1000 ADA Compliance | © 2011 Ohio University. All rights reserved. uhr@ohio.edu To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
